-## 11. Optional: a quick-scan list (a prompt to revise, not a find-and-replace)
+## 11. Pep-talk closers, engagement bait, and call-to-action clichés
 
+Posts, essays, and newsletters generated by a model end on a push: a motivational line, a question aimed at comments, an urge to act today. They say nothing the piece has not already said, and they appear on schedule, which is why they read as machine-made.
+
+**Pep-talk closers:** "Don't be afraid to fail." "Every step brings you closer to mastery." "The best time to start is now." "Believe in yourself and anything is possible." "Your journey starts today."
+**Engagement bait:** "What's holding you back?" "What goal are you chasing?" "Agree?" "Thoughts?" "Drop a comment below." "Tag someone who needs this."
+**Take-the-first-step clichés:** "Take the first step today." "If you're thinking about making a leap, start now."
+
+**Before:** The first three months were rough, and we lost money every week. Then the Saturday subscription took off. If you're thinking about making a leap of your own, the best time to start is now. What's holding you back?
+**After:** The first three months were rough, and we lost money every week. Then the Saturday subscription took off.
+
+Fix: cut the push and end on the last concrete thing the draft says. If the draft's closer carries something real (a link, a sign-up date, a specific ask), keep that and say it plainly. If the draft is a personal post and the author wants to leave the reader with a lesson, state the lesson they actually drew, in their words, instead of a slogan about the reader's future. Never replace a cut closer with a new claim.
+
+---
+
+## 12. Optional: a quick-scan list (a prompt to revise, not a find-and-replace)
+
 This is a convenience, not the method. The whole skill exists because swapping words off a list produces flat, evenly paced, voiceless prose that still flags as AI. Use this list only to *locate* suspect passages fast; then do the real structural and voice work from sections 5 through 10. A hit is not automatically wrong. It is a place to look, and context decides.
 
 If you specifically want an exhaustive, tiered banned-word workflow (severity levels, context profiles, a flag-only mode), that is a different tool: the `avoid-ai-writing` skill owns it. This list stays deliberately short.
